An official version that is released on a more regular basis, so that
players don't have to wait a year or more to avoid bugs and enjoy new
features, and so that much time isn't wasted on multifarious spinoffs,
which include many good ideas that frequently don't get into the
stable line of official releases for reasons usually unknown to the
coder and player community, because the "devteam" doesn't communicate
very well.

Communication and slowness are the essential problems with the way
nethack is currently developed.  We're given a web site where bugs are
identified and described as "fixed", but for 99% of them we end up
waiting months or years anyway to use the actual fix, again in the
"official" versions, like some huge programming cocktease.  The
hodgepodge of patches and spinoffs, again while a source of good
ideas, are distracting and annoying to use.  A much better scenario
would be a devteam willing to release their iron grip on "officialdom"
and turn the project over to one of the collaborative programming
sites, if they are unwilling to move their solutions along any faster.

"The devteam has put thousands of hours into coding, so we should only
scrape and bow and prostrate ourselves" line has worn thin.  There are
hundreds, probably thousands of well-run programming projects, by
volunteers, which fix bugs and put releases out more regularly, and
communicate much better with their user communities.  The
"nethack/devteam/high priests of isolation and beneficence" model is
outdated.


